Link a static executable by a GNU ld script, the file -Wl,-T or
-Wl,--script names.

A kernel linked at an address in the top of the address space and loaded
at a low physical one takes its layout from a script: the address of
each section, its load address, the program headers, and symbols such as
the end of the image by physical address. TinyCC 0.9.27 read a script
only for INPUT and GROUP and placed the sections itself, the data
segment a page further from the code in memory than in the file. This
reads the part of the script language such a layout has, and lays a
static executable out by it:

  * ENTRY; symbol assignments, at the top and in SECTIONS, with `.` among
    them, one at the top after SECTIONS taken once the sections before it
    are laid out; PHDRS with PT_LOAD, FILEHDR, PHDRS and FLAGS; output
    sections with an address, AT(), a list of input section patterns and
    assignments, and :phdr; and /DISCARD/.
  * Expressions of numbers, with K or M after them, symbols the script
    assigned before, `.`, the operators of C from unary minus and ~ to |,
    and ALIGN, ADDR, LOADADDR, SIZEOF and SIZEOF_HEADERS, the last counted
    from PHDRS, anywhere after it.

An allocated section the script names nowhere, as TinyCC's own .got,
joins the end of the last output section of its kind, code, read-only
data, data or .bss. A section lies in the file as far from its segment's
start as it lies in memory, and a segment starts at the first file offset
past the one before that is congruent to its address modulo the section
alignment, -Wl,-section-alignment, as GNU ld lays one out with its
maximum page size; the first, with the headers at file offset 0, at the
address its first section's less the headers' size rounds down to, so
that the first section may lie past the headers by what its alignment
asks. What an output section reserves after its last section, by
advancing `.`, is memory of its segment. Where the script's sections follow one another in
memory, the file then holds them as memory does, which the a.out kludge
of a Multiboot header needs: its loader copies the file to memory whole.
The script's symbols are absolute, and take the place of any other
definition, TinyCC's own _etext and _end among them. They are in the
symbol table before the GOT is built and the table's size is fixed, so a
reference to one, a PC-relative one among them, reaches it and no PLT
entry, and a link with -g writes them.

Anything else in a script is an error, not something passed over. A link
without a script is unchanged.

